# Normalise line endings so a checkout on one platform cannot commit a
# different encoding than a checkout on another.
#
# Without this, Git only knows what bytes it was handed. During the work
# on #65 a single CRLF reached tests/reexports.rs from a Windows editor
# and was committed; it survived review and was only caught by
# inspecting `git ls-files --eol`. Nothing in CI would have failed.
#
# `text=auto` lets Git detect which files are text and leaves genuine
# binaries untouched, so no explicit `binary` entries are needed here.
# `eol=lf` additionally forces LF in the *working tree*, not just the
# index — that is the part that stops an editor round-tripping a file
# back to CRLF on Windows.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Called by CI on ubuntu-latest. CRLF here would break the shebang and
# fail with a confusing "bad interpreter" error rather than a diff.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Generated by device-driver-cli from tmp108.ddsl and committed to the
# tree; regenerating it on a different platform must not produce a
# whole-file diff.
src/inner.rs text eol=lf
